        "title": "Nutrient Cycling: Nitrogen Fixation, Nitrification, Phosphorus Cycle",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Soil nutrient cycling is the engine of terrestrial productivity — the biochemical machinery that converts atmospheric gases and mineral elements into plant-available forms and back again. Nitrogen and phosphorus are the most commonly limiting nutrients, but their cycling pathways are fundamentally different: nitrogen cycles through gas phases (atmospheric N₂ pool; N₂O and N₂ losses), while phosphorus is entirely sedimentary (no atmospheric pool; driven by weathering and biological transformation)."
    },
